window.docContents[43].resolve({"/#combining-io-actions":{"contents":"Most useful programs accept input in addition to producing output.\nFurthermore, they may take decisions based on input, using the input data as part of a computation.\nThe following program, called HelloName.lean, asks the user for their name and then greets them:\n\n\n\nIn this program, the  action consists of a do block.\nThis block contains a sequence of statements, which can be both local variables (introduced using let) and actions that are to be executed.\nJust as SQL can be thought of as a special-purpose language for interacting with databases, the do syntax can be thought of as a special-purpose sub-language within Lean that is dedicated to modeling imperative programs.\n actions that are built with a do block are executed by executing the statements in order.\n\nThis program can be run in the same manner as the prior program:\n\nlean --run HelloName.lean\n\nIf the user responds with David, a session of interaction with the program reads:\n\n\n\nThe type signature line is just like the one for Hello.lean:\n\n\n\nThe only difference is that it ends with the keyword , which initiates a sequence of commands.\nEach indented line following the keyword do is part of the same sequence of commands.\n\nThe first two lines, which read:\n\n\n\nretrieve the  and  handles by executing the library actions  and , respectively.\nIn a  block,  has a slightly different meaning than in an ordinary expression.\nOrdinarily, the local definition in a  can be used in just one expression, which immediately follows the local definition.\nIn a  block, local bindings introduced by  are available in all statements in the remainder of the  block, rather than just the next one.\nAdditionally,  typically connects the name being defined to its definition using :=, while some  bindings in  use a left arrow (← or <-) instead.\nUsing an arrow means that the value of the expression is an  action that should be executed, with the result of the action saved in the local variable.\nIn other words, if the expression to the right of the arrow has type , then the variable has type  in the remainder of the  block.\n and  are  actions in order to allow  and  to be locally overridden in a program, which can be convenient.\nIf they were global variables as in C, then there would be no meaningful way to override them, but  actions can return different values each time they are executed.\n\nThe next part of the  block is responsible for asking the user for their name:\n\n\n\nThe first line writes the question to , the second line requests input from , and the third line removes the trailing newline (plus any other trailing whitespace) from the input line.\nThe definition of  uses :=, rather than ←, because  is an ordinary function on strings, rather than an  action.\n\nFinally, the last line in the program is:\n\n\n\nIt uses string interpolation to insert the provided name into a greeting string, writing the result to .\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Hello, World!\u0009Running a Program","header":"2.1.4. Combining  IO  Actions","id":"/#combining-io-actions"}});